					{
					"id": "beat_1",
					"title": "Free-Body Diagrams and Equilibrium",
					"narration": "Begin by isolating any structural component and replacing all external connections with their equivalent forces and moments. This process creates a free-body diagram that visually captures every external load acting on the body. Once drawn, enforce translational equilibrium by summing all horizontal and vertical forces to zero, and enforce rotational equilibrium by summing all moments about a chosen pivot to zero. These equilibrium conditions allow you to solve for unknown reactions or applied loads. The diagram must show every force vector with its direction, magnitude, and point of application, ensuring that the mathematical system accurately reflects the physical reality of the structure. This foundational step prevents sign errors and establishes a clear reference frame for all subsequent calculations.",
