					"id": "beat_2",
					"title": "Internal Resultants and Section Cuts",
					"narration": "To understand how a structure responds internally, make a conceptual cut through the member and expose the hidden forces that keep the two halves in equilibrium. These exposed forces are the internal resultants, which include axial forces, shear forces, and bending moments. The direction of each internal resultant is determined by Newton's third law: the cut face must exert forces that balance the external loads on the exposed segment. By analyzing either side of the cut, you can compute the exact magnitude and direction of these internal forces. This technique transforms a complex structural problem into manageable equilibrium equations, revealing how loads are distributed throughout the material rather than acting only at the boundaries.",
					"visualDirection": "A split rectangular beam showing a vertical cut through its center. The left half displays external loads and reaction forces, while the right half shows the exposed internal force","plannedSeconds": 65,
					"claimIds": ["mit-solid-mechanics"],"accessibility": {
						"describedInNarration": true,
						"audioDescriptionCue": "A beam split vertically at the center, with internal forces labeled on the cut face and equilibrium equations shown for each segment."
						}
