					{
					"id": "beat_3",
					"title": "Normal Stress and Shear Stress",
					"narration": "Stress quantifies how internal forces are distributed across a material's cross-section rather than treating them as single point loads. Normal stress measures the intensity of axial forces perpendicular to a chosen section, calculated by dividing the axial force by the cross-sectional area. Shear stress measures the intensity of internal forces parallel to that section, which resist sliding between adjacent material layers. The orientation of the section matters significantly, as the same internal force produces different stress values depending on how you slice the member. By explicitly defining the section plane and applying the correct force component, you ensure that the calculated stress accurately represents the actual material intensity at that location.",
					"visualDirection": "A rectangular bar with a horizontal cut through its cross-section. The cut face shows normal stress arrows pointing outward and shear stress arrows pointing parallel to the cut,",
					"plannedSeconds": 60,
					"claimIds": ["mit-solid-mechanics"],"accessibility": {
						"describedInNarration": true,
						"audioDescriptionCue": "A bar with a horizontal cut showing normal stress arrows perpendicular to the cut face and shear stress arrows parallel to it."
						}
					},
					{
					"id": "beat_4",
					"title": "Normal Strain and Elastic Modulus",
					"narration": "When a member is loaded, it deforms, and this deformation is measured as strain, which represents the fractional change in length relative to the original length. Normal strain quantifies elongation or shortening along the loading axis. The relationship between normal stress and normal strain is governed by the material's elastic modulus, which acts as a stiffness coefficient that links force intensity to deformation intensity. Within a specific range, the material responds predictably, meaning that doubling the stress produces exactly double the strain. This linear relationship allows engineers to predict how much a component will stretch or compress under known loads, providing a direct bridge between mechanical forces and physical deformation.",
					"visualDirection": "A bar with a grid of vertical lines representing original length. The bar is stretched, and the spacing between lines increases. A formula showing stress equals elastic modulus",
					"plannedSeconds": 65,
					"claimIds": ["mit-materials-structures"],"accessibility": {
						"describedInNarration": true,
						"audioDescriptionCue": "A bar with a grid of vertical lines representing original length. The bar is stretched, and the spacing between lines increases. A formula showing stress equals elastic modulus times strain."
						}
					},
					{
					"id": "beat_5",
					"title": "Linear Elasticity and Stress-Strain Curves",
					"narration": "Linear elasticity describes the region where stress and strain are directly proportional, forming a straight line on a stress-strain curve. This straight-line behavior persists only up to a specific stress threshold, beyond which the material's response becomes nonlinear. The slope of this straight line represents the elastic modulus, which remains constant throughout the elastic region. By plotting stress against strain, you can visually identify the elastic limit and understand how the material transitions from predictable deformation to more complex behavior. This graphical representation is essential for selecting appropriate materials and ensuring that design loads remain within the safe, linear range where predictable deformation occurs.",
					"visualDirection": "A graph with stress on the y-axis and strain on the x-axis. A straight line starts from the origin, labeled with the elastic modulus, and bends at a point labeled 'elastic limit'.",
					"plannedSeconds": 60,
					"claimIds": ["mit-materials-structures"],"accessibility": {
						"describedInNarration": true,
						"audioDescriptionCue": "A stress-strain graph showing a straight line from the origin labeled with the elastic modulus, bending at the elastic limit."
						}
					},
					{
					"id": "beat_6",
					"title": "Yielding and Plasticity",
					"narration": "Yielding marks the point where a material begins to deform permanently, meaning it will not return to its original shape when the load is removed. This transition occurs when the applied stress exceeds the material's yield strength, causing internal atomic bonds to slide past one another. Once yielding begins, the stress-strain curve bends, and the material enters a plastic region where deformation increases rapidly with relatively small additional stress. Understanding yielding is critical because permanent deformation can alter the geometry of a structure, potentially leading to instability or reduced load capacity. Engineers must ensure that design stresses remain below the yield point to preserve the intended shape and function of the component.",
