					{
					"id": "beat_7",
					"title": "Fracture and Failure Mechanisms",
					"narration": "Fracture occurs when the material can no longer sustain the applied stress, resulting in sudden rupture or crack propagation. Unlike yielding, which involves permanent but reversible deformation, fracture represents complete structural failure. The stress-strain curve typically shows a sharp drop in stress after the ultimate strength is reached, indicating that the material can no longer carry additional load. Fatigue is another failure mechanism where repeated cyclic loading causes cracks to initiate and grow over time, even if the stress remains below the yield point. Recognizing these failure modes helps engineers design components that can withstand both static and dynamic loading conditions without catastrophic breakdown.",
