					"title": "Safety Factors and Design Checks",
					"narration": "Safety factors provide a margin of protection between the predicted mechanical demands and the material's or structure's failure limits. By comparing the calculated stress or force to the material's yield strength or fracture limit, you determine whether the design is adequate. A higher safety factor reduces the risk of failure but may increase material usage and cost. Design checks require explicit comparison of these values, ensuring that the actual demands remain within the acceptable range. This systematic evaluation process transforms theoretical calculations into reliable engineering decisions, balancing performance requirements with structural integrity and material efficiency.",
